I rewrote /Users/tonioloewald/tjs-lang/guides/sections/ajs-api.md. The metadata line and `<!-- TODO review -->` are unchanged, and so is the toc block.

**What changed:**
- **Title:** now "Agents that call APIs".
- **Opening:** says the example pages run live, with the doc site as the host passing each `vm.run` an allowlist of three domains.
- **New section, "The network is something the host grants":**
  - explains that `httpFetch` is an atom with no network access of its own;
  - says what a capability is and that "run" means one `vm.run` call;
  - shows a non-runnable `javascript` host snippet with the two grants: `context: { allowedFetchDomains }` and `capabilities: { fetch }`;
  - says how the two combine: the allowlist still applies to a custom fetch, and with no allowlist the host's fetch decides where requests go;
  - quotes the refusal messages as prefixes, including the separate messages for private addresses and for non-http(s) schemes;
  - says allowlist entries are exact: `*.example.com` admits subdomains and `example.com` itself, and `host:port` admits another port.
- **The three example descriptions:**
  - Defines "not an AJS error": a 404 or 500 comes back as data the agent cannot catch, with a link to `/ajs-error-handling/`.
  - Adds that redirects come back as `{ redirect: true, status, location }`.
  - Says why size matters: `membraneMaxBytes` (4 MB default; an oversized body is refused, not truncated), fuel, and model context.
- **Closing:** links to `/ajs-host/#fetch-policy`.

**One finding was wrong in its suggested fix.** It proposed saying iTunes hands back a string you must `JSON.parse`. The itunes-search page actually uses `responseType: 'json'`, because `httpFetch` parses a body on its own only when it is labeled `application/json`. The page now says that.

**How I checked it:** I ran the fetch behaviour through transpile and AgentVM, using `t.ts` in `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-tonioloewald-tjs-lang/ceca4d7c-a13f-43be-92e8-1e916ca662c7/scratchpad/docs-swarm/ajs-api/`. These cases came out as the page says:
- no policy, localhost and an ftp URL each produced their own refusal message;
- a custom fetch with no allowlist was passed the request;
- a custom fetch with an allowlist was still refused for a domain not on the list;
- `example.com` did not admit `api.example.com` or port 8443, and `*.example.com` did admit the subdomain;
- a live open-meteo call with the allowlist returned data.

The redirect and oversize claims I checked against the runtime source (the `httpFetch` default path and `readBounded`), not by running them.

